{"qid":"Q732738","names":["County of Maine"],"title":"County of Maine","endonym":null,"capsule":"The County of Maine was a Frankish county in northwestern France, with its capital at Le Mans, strategically positioned between Normandy and Anjou. William the Conqueror seized it by 1064, and its subsequent inheritance by the Plantagenets made it a central component of the Angevin Empire; Henry II of England held it as count alongside his continental domains. In 1204 Philip II of France swept through the region following King John's loss of Normandy, and in 1481 the county was formally bequeathed to the French crown by Charles IV of Maine, definitively ending its separate existence.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The county's borders are reasonably well attested through feudal charters and Capetian administrative records, though exact limits varied as control shifted between Normandy, Anjou, and France.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Maine (province)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maine_(province)"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Angevin Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angevin_Empire"},{"label":"Britannica: Maine","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Maine-historical-province-France"}],"britannica":"place/Maine-historical-region-France","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Maine (province)","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Hugh III","nativeName":"Hugues III","roman":"Hugh III","start":990,"end":992,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Herbert I Wake-Dog","nativeName":"Herbert Éveille-Chien","roman":"Herbert I","start":992,"end":1035,"image":null,"imageUrl"